.TH CHTTP_REQUEST_SET_HEADER 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttp_request_set_header \- set a request header, or replace one
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpclient.h>

ccol_retval_t chttp_request_set_header(chttp_request_t *req,
                                       const char *name,
                                       const char *value);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This function puts the header with the name that you give into the
request, or replaces it. The request stores each header name in
lowercase. Every later lookup therefore ignores the case of the name.
If a header with the same name is already there, the function writes
the new value over the old one. The match of the name ignores the
case.

The library makes the map of the headers on the first call.

name must be a token that is not empty, and that holds only tchar
bytes of RFC 7230 SS3.2.6. Every byte must be an ASCII letter, an
ASCII digit, or one of "!#$%&'*+-.^_`|~". A name of zero length has no
valid form on the wire. A name with any other byte in it, such as a
space or a literal ':', is not a way to inject a CRLF. But it still
makes a line on the wire with a broken structure. A strict server or
proxy further down can read such a line in the wrong way.

value must not hold a CR byte or an LF byte inside it. The serializer
writes "name: value\er\en" onto the wire with no escape. A caller can
reflect untrusted data into a header, such as a token that it forwards
or a header from a proxy. Without this check, a CR or an LF in value
lets that caller inject any number of extra header lines. It also lets
the caller cut the request into two, which is HTTP request splitting.
The function therefore gives ccol_invalid_args for such a name or
value, and stores nothing.

The function also rejects a name of "Transfer-Encoding", and the match
of that name ignores the case. chttpclient never applies a transfer
coding to a request body. It always sends a request with a body whole,
and frames it with Content-Length. Without this rejection, the
automatic Content-Length of chttpclient_do puts a Content-Length
header beside the Transfer-Encoding header on the wire. The body
then carries no transfer coding. That framing is ambiguous, and the
request and response parser of this library rejects such a framing in
a message that it parses (RFC 7230 SS3.3.3).

The function DOES accept a "Content-Length" header. It has no body
length to check the value against. A check here also needs a copy of
the method and of the redirect context of the request, which come
later. The client validates the header later, when the request reaches
the wire. A request that carries a body uses the method POST, PUT or
PATCH. On such a request, chttpclient_do and every other function
that runs a request reject a bad Content-Length from the caller. A
good value is a plain unsigned string
of decimal digits. It has no leading '+' or '-', and no whitespace
inside it. It must also match the body that the client sends exactly.
The reason is the same reason that the client rejects
Transfer-Encoding above: the declared framing and the wire must not
disagree. See chttpclient_do(3).

A header that you set here follows every redirect, with two
exceptions. A hop whose scheme, host or port differs from the original
request carries no Authorization, Cookie, Cookie2 or WWW-Authenticate
header that you set. A hop whose host differs carries no Host header
that you set, and the client writes its own. Each hop is compared with
the original request, so a hop back to the original origin carries
them again. A host compares without regard to case. See
chttpclient_do(3).

The client writes a default value for Host, Accept, User-Agent,
Content-Length, Content-Type, Authorization and Expect when it needs
one. When you set a header with one of these names here, the client
does not write the default. The client sends your value exactly as you
gave it. This is also true for Host.

Only POST, PUT and PATCH have a body. For all other methods, the client
does not send a Content-Length, Content-Type or Expect header that you
set. This is also true on a hop that a 301, 302 or 303 redirect changed
into a GET. The reason is that there is no body for the header to
describe.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
req     The request to change. It must not be NULL.
name    The header name, for example "Authorization". It must not be
        NULL and must not be empty. It must hold only tchar bytes of
        RFC 7230. It must not be "Transfer-Encoding".
value   The header value. It must not be NULL. It must not hold a CR
        byte or an LF byte.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success            The request has the header.
ccol_invalid_args       req, name or value is NULL. Or name is empty,
                        or holds a byte outside the tchar set of RFC
                        7230. Or value holds a CR byte or an LF byte
                        inside it. Or name is "Transfer-Encoding".
ccol_not_enough_memory  The allocation of the map failed.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chttp_request_get_header (3)
